From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 01:47:06 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: move the shared PCIe wiring into the AVM dtsi

The comment on this block named its own precondition: it would stay in
the FRITZ!Box 7560's board file until a second board had actually
enumerated a device behind one of these ports. The FRITZ!Box 7590 is
about to, so it moves.

Nothing about the moved half is board-specific. Both boards drive PERST#
from gpio0 9 and gpio0 8, active low; both enable all three serdes PHYs
with spread spectrum on; both swap inbound and leave outbound alone.
That is not a coincidence between two boards either -- the vendor states
these once, in a file every FRITZ!Box on this SoC includes, and no board
file anywhere in its tree overrides any of it.

The claim the move makes, stated so it can be checked later: every AVM
board on this SoC puts a radio behind RC0 and RC1. The FRITZ!Box 7580 is
the next board that would test it, and if one ever turns up with a port
unused, a board file can disable it again -- the claim is about what
this family does, not about what is possible.

Two things deliberately do not move. RC2 stays per-board because what
sits behind it differs -- a VRX318 on the FRITZ!Box 7560, a VRX518 on
the FRITZ!Box 7590 -- and the two want opposite inbound byte swapping as
a result, which is the single largest PCIe difference between the
boards. And the FRITZ!Box 7560's ath9k endpoint node stays with the
board that has the part; what it describes is a blank EEPROM on one
specific radio, not a port. Its port is now enabled in the dtsi and the
board file adds only the child, which dtc merges into the same node.

The PHYs all three move together, RC2's included. They are three
instances of one block with nothing board-specific to say, and a powered
PHY with no root complex behind it does nothing.

seale_avm.dtsi gains the gpio dt-bindings include the moved reset-gpios
need. Both board files already pull it in ahead of this one, so nothing
changes today; the include is there so the file does not depend on its
includers for a macro it uses itself.

The FRITZ!Box 7560's DTB is unchanged by this, byte for byte.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
index 0aecb66c3d94..2900f83e4de4 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
@@ -7,6 +7,8 @@
  * per-board data is in the board .dts.
  */
 
+#include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+
 #include "xrx500.dtsi"
 
 /*
@@ -41,3 +43,38 @@ reserved-memory {
 		ranges;
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * All three serdes PHYs and the two root complexes that carry radios. PERST#
+ * is active low on every port, spread-spectrum clocking is on, and inbound
+ * byte swapping is on because the SoC is big-endian and the DesignWare core
+ * is not.
+ */
+&pcie_phy0 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
+};
+
+&pcie_phy1 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
+};
+
+&pcie_phy2 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
+};
+
+&pcie0 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,inbound-swap = <1>;
+	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 9 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+};
+
+&pcie1 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,inbound-swap = <1>;
+	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 8 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+};
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
index 330f344e100a..0b22a44335f3 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
@@ -236,58 +236,11 @@ dect-reset-hog {
 	};
 };
 
-&pcie_phy0 {
-	status = "okay";
-	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
-};
-
-&pcie_phy1 {
-	status = "okay";
-	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
-};
-
-&pcie_phy2 {
-	status = "okay";
-	intel,ssc-enable = <1>;
-};
-
 /*
- * Inbound byte swapping is enabled on the two radio ports and disabled
- * on RC2. This SoC is big endian and the DWC core is not, so inbound
- * DMA is byte-swapped on the way through -- except for the VRX318,
- * whose driver already presents its shared memory in the endianness the
- * host expects, and swapping again would undo it. The vendor tree
- * arrives at the same split: grx_common.dtsi sets inbound-swap on all
- * three, and vrx318.dtsi, included only by boards carrying that chip,
- * clears it on RC2.
- *
- * Outbound is never swapped, on any port.
+ * The 2.4 GHz radio, behind RC1. qca,no-eeprom is required: there is no
+ * calibration EEPROM on the card and the driver must take it from userspace.
  */
-&pcie0 {
-	status = "okay";
-	intel,inbound-swap = <1>;
-	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
-	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 9 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
-};
-
 &pcie1 {
-	status = "okay";
-	intel,inbound-swap = <1>;
-	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
-	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 8 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
-
-	/*
-	 * The 2.4 GHz radio is an AR93xx whose EEPROM is blank, so it
-	 * enumerates as 168c:abcd (AR9300_DEVID_INVALID). ath9k_of_init()
-	 * only looks for calibration data in a file when the device has an
-	 * OF node carrying qca,no-eeprom, so without this node the driver
-	 * keeps AH_USE_EEPROM and the radio cannot come up at all -- there
-	 * is nothing on the part to fall back to.
-	 *
-	 * The outer node is the root port at devfn 0 on bus 0, the inner
-	 * one the endpoint at devfn 0 on bus 1; matching is by devfn, so
-	 * the compatible is documentation.
-	 */
 	pcie@0 {
 		reg = <0 0 0 0 0>;
 		#size-cells = <2>;
@@ -304,7 +257,10 @@ wifi@0,0 {
 	};
 };
 
-/* VRX318 DSL companion -- see the inbound-swap note above. */
+/*
+ * VRX318 DSL companion, behind RC2. Inbound byte swapping is off: the
+ * endpoint's driver already presents host-endian shared memory.
+ */
 &pcie2 {
 	status = "okay";
 	intel,inbound-swap = <0>;
